---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { catalog } from '../lib/catalog';

const data = catalog();
---

<Base
  title="The format — PhoenixSEO Components"
  description="A component is a directory: definition.json, an optional render.php and an optional component.css. Here is how the three fit together and when you need which."
  path="/format/"
>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <header class="head">
      <p class="eyebrow">The format</p>
      <h1>A component is a directory</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Three files at most, no class to extend, no plugin to write. What the editor shows, what the
        page stores and what the browser gets are three different things, and the directory is
        where they meet.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="three-files">
      <h2 id="three-files">Three files, one directory</h2>
      <pre><code>{`sdc-components/<type>/
    definition.json    required — fields, title, icon
    render.php         optional — markup
    component.css      optional — presentation`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The directory name <em>is</em> the type. It appears in the field group key
        (<code>group_sdcpb_component_&lt;type&gt;</code>), in the block name
        (<code>sdcpb/&lt;type&gt;</code>) and in the path — so lowercase letters, digits and
        hyphens only. An underscore is a valid component type but not a valid block name, and
        WordPress rejects <code>sdcpb/feature_grid</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>render.php</code> receives three variables: <code>$type</code>, <code>$data</code> (the
        raw values) and <code>$component</code> (the entry from the page document). It prints its
        own markup, including the wrapping element.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="where">
      <h2 id="where">Where a component may live</h2>
      <p>
        The same four places are searched for the definition, the template and the stylesheet, in
        this order. The first match wins, so a child theme overrides the parent theme, both override
        <code>wp-content/sdc-components/</code>, and the plugin's shipped examples are the last
        resort.
      </p>
      <ol class="paths">
        <li><code>child theme/sdc-components/</code></li>
        <li><code>parent theme/sdc-components/</code></li>
        <li><code>wp-content/sdc-components/</code> — editable in the admin, survives updates</li>
        <li><code>plugin/schemas/</code> — replaced with every plugin update</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="small soft">
        To change a shipped component, copy it into <code>wp-content/sdc-components/</code>. Editing
        the plugin's copy works until the next update overwrites it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="enough">
      <h2 id="enough">When <code>definition.json</code> is enough</h2>
      <p>
        Without a <code>render.php</code> the plugin prints the fields generically: each field as a
        labelled block, lists as rows. That is usable for FAQs, addresses and tables — anywhere the
        order of the fields already is the statement.
      </p>
      <p>Custom markup pays off as soon as one of these holds:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The element carries a meaning that fields cannot express
          (<code>&lt;blockquote&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;dl&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;figure&gt;</code>).
        </li>
        <li>A field value is a key that has to be translated first — an icon name, a colour tone.</li>
        <li>The number of entries decides the layout: one tier or five.</li>
        <li>A rule spans several rows, like exactly one highlighted tier.</li>
        <li>Nobody should type a value that can be computed: a track number, a total, a count.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="helpers">
      <h2 id="helpers">The helpers in <code>render.php</code></h2>
      <p>
        All of them print, and all of them escape. <code>sdcpb_value()</code> is the way past the
        escaping to the raw value.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Helper</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="mono small">sdcpb_text( 'field' )</td><td>value as text</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono small">sdcpb_html( 'field' )</td><td>value as HTML, through <code>wp_kses_post</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono small">sdcpb_attr( 'field' ), sdcpb_url( 'field' )</td><td>value inside an attribute</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono small">sdcpb_has( 'field' )</td><td>does the field hold a value? <code>'0'</code> counts as one</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono small">sdcpb_link( 'field', 'class' )</td><td>an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> from a link field</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono small">sdcpb_image( 'field', 'large' )</td><td>an <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> with alt, width and height</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono small">sdcpb_image_style( 'field' )</td><td><code>style="--sdc-image:url(…)"</code> for backgrounds</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono small">sdcpb_each( 'list', fn )</td><td>the rows of a repeater</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono small">sdcpb_fields()</td><td>the generic markup, when only the frame is custom</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Inside <code>sdcpb_each()</code> every helper reads the component, not the row. The row comes
        along as the last argument: <code>sdcpb_text( 'name', $row )</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="styles">
      <h2 id="styles">Styles</h2>
      <p>
        <code>component.css</code> is loaded automatically as soon as the component appears on the
        page — nothing to register. It is inlined into the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, not linked as a
        separate file; set <code>sdcpb_inline_component_styles</code> to <code>false</code> to get
        separate files.
      </p>
      <p>
        The examples work with CSS variables and <code>color-mix()</code> so they stay readable in any
        theme and can be overridden with a single line:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`.sdc-feature-grid { --sdc-fg-min: 18rem; }
.sdc-cta { --sdc-cta-bg: #0f766e; --sdc-cta-fg: #ffffff; }`}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="start">
      <h2 id="start">Start with one of these</h2>
      <p>
        The {data.components.length} components in the catalog double as a course in the format:
        <a href="/components/hero/">hero</a> and <a href="/components/faq/">faq</a> need no template at
        all, <a href="/components/vinyl/">vinyl</a> and <a href="/components/series/">series</a> show
        what a template buys you.
      </p>
      <p><a href="/components/">Browse the catalog</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem) 2rem;
  }

  .head h1 {
    margin-block: 0.5rem 0.85rem;
    max-width: 26ch;
  }

  section + section {
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
  }

  h2 {
    margin-bottom: 0.85rem;
  }

  h2 code {
    font-size: 0.8em;
  }

  .prose p,
  .prose ul,
  .prose ol {
    max-width: 66ch;
  }

  .prose pre {
    margin-block: 0.5rem 1rem;
    max-width: 66ch;
  }

  .prose ul {
    padding-inline-start: 1.15rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.3rem;
  }

  .paths {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.3rem;
    padding-inline-start: 1.15rem;
    margin-block: 0.75rem;
  }

  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    margin-block: 0.85rem;
  }

  .table td:first-child {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
